            <li>Web Developers (NOC 21234)</li>
            <li>Database Analysts (NOC 21223)</li>
            <li>Systems Analysts (NOC 21222)</li>
            <li>UX/UI Designers (NOC 21233)</li>
            <li>QA Engineers (NOC 21232)</li>
            <li>IT Project Managers (NOC 20012)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Salaries for these roles in British Columbia are competitive, with senior and principal engineers commanding $150,000 to $200,000+ annually, often with stock options. Vancouver's tech ecosystem includes major employers like Amazon, Microsoft, SAP, Slack, and hundreds of startups — plus a growing cluster of AI-focused companies drawn by proximity to the University of British Columbia's world-class research programs.
      </p>

      <!-- Section 7 -->
      <h2 id="action-plan">Your Action Plan: From Layoff to Landing in BC</h2>

      <p>
        If you're a tech worker who's been laid off — or you can see the writing on the wall — here's a concrete roadmap for turning disruption into opportunity:
      </p>

      <h3>Step 1: Calculate Your BC PNP Score</h3>
      <p>
        Before anything else, find out where you stand. The <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator">BC PNP Calculator</a> will estimate your Skills Immigration Registration System (SIRS) score based on your education, work experience, language ability, and job offer details. The latest Innovate points cutoff (October 29, 2026) was 131 — know your number.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 2: Secure a Job Offer from a BC Employer</h3>
      <p>
        A valid job offer is the single biggest factor in your BC PNP application. There is no longer tech priority processing; a genuine offer in a TEER 0 to 3 role that meets the Innovate wage line, or a strong score, is what gets you invited. Remote-first companies with Canadian legal entities count. Start with LinkedIn, Indeed Canada, and specialized Canadian tech job boards. Many BC employers are LMIA-exempt under the International Mobility Program, which means faster processing for your work permit.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 3: Stack Your Applications</h3>
      <p>
        Don't put all your eggs in one basket. You can simultaneously register for BC PNP (Skills Immigration or Express Entry BC), submit an Express Entry profile for Canadian Experience Class or category rounds, and explore other provincial programs in Ontario (OINP) or Saskatchewan (SINP). Multiple active applications multiply your chances without any downside.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 4: Get Your Language Test Done Early</h3>
      <p>
        IELTS or CELPIP scores are required and take time. A CLB 7+ in all categories significantly boosts your points. Don't wait until you have a job offer — book your test now and have scores ready. Check our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-language-requirements">language requirements guide</a> for details.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 5: Time It Right</h3>
      <p>
        BC's 2026 allocation rose to 6,254 in August, and Innovate thresholds eased through the year: points from 138 to 131 and the wage line from $70 to $49 an hour. BC's 2027 allocation has not been announced, and early-year rounds have been smaller in both 2025 and 2026, so register while your profile is current rather than waiting for a better moment.
      </p>

