      <!-- Section 8: FAQ -->
      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <h3>Can I apply for Canadian PR if I was laid off from a tech job due to AI?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. Being laid off does not disqualify you from Canadian immigration. If you have a valid job offer from a Canadian employer, you can apply through BC PNP, Express Entry, or other provincial nominee programs. Your previous work experience still counts toward your eligibility and points.
      </p>

      <h3>What is the BC PNP Tech stream and how does it help tech workers?</h3>
      <p>
        The BC PNP no longer runs tech draws; the final priority tech draw was December 3, 2024. The former tech occupations remain eligible, and many can still qualify with a limited-term job offer of at least one year. Tech workers now compete in Innovate (High Economic Impact) rounds: on October 29, 2026, the wage track required $49 an hour and $99,000 a year in a TEER 0 to 3 job, and the points track required 131.
      </p>

      <h3>How many PNP spots does Canada have for 2026?</h3>
      <p>
        Canada's 2026 PNP target is 91,500 admissions, a 66% increase from 55,000 in 2025, and the 2027–2029 plan raises it to 95,000 a year. British Columbia's 2026 allocation started at 5,254 and rose to 6,254 on August 18, 2026. The PNP is about 38% of planned 2026 economic immigration.
      </p>

      <h3>Is it harder to get a tech job in Canada in 2026 because of AI?</h3>
      <p>
        The global market is tougher, but Canada's tech sector continues to grow — particularly in Vancouver, Toronto, and Montreal. BC no longer runs tech-specific draws, but tech workers can be invited through Innovate rounds. Senior developers, AI/ML engineers, cybersecurity specialists, and cloud architects remain in high demand. Salaries for senior roles range from $150,000 to $200,000+.
      </p>

      <h3>What is the minimum BC PNP score for tech workers in 2026?</h3>
      <p>
        There are no separate tech draws. Innovate points cutoffs ranged from 138 in February 2026 to 131 in September and October, and the wage track required $49 an hour and $99,000 a year on October 29, 2026. <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator">Use our calculator</a> to estimate your score and see where you stand relative to recent cutoffs.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I apply to multiple provinces at the same time?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. You can register for BC PNP, Ontario's OINP, Saskatchewan's SINP, and submit a federal Express Entry profile simultaneously. There's no restriction against having multiple active applications, and this strategy significantly increases your chances of receiving an invitation. Check our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-ontario-oinp-2026">BC PNP vs Ontario comparison</a> for details.
      </p>

      <!-- Bottom Line -->
      <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

      <p>
        The AI disruption reshaping the global tech industry is real, and the job losses are painful. But buried inside that disruption is an opportunity that most people are overlooking entirely. Canada — and British Columbia in particular — is actively competing for the exact talent that other countries and companies are discarding.
      </p>

      <p>
        The numbers are more favorable than in 2025: 91,500 PNP admissions nationally in 2026 and 95,000 a year planned for 2027 to 2029, a BC allocation raised to 6,254, and Innovate thresholds that eased through 2026. The dedicated tech draws are gone, so the route now runs through a strong wage, a strong score, or both.
